  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "abb9ba34-017c-50b2-ac29-8b64443fc19c",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-6 & =6x-7 \\\\ 6x-6-6x & =6x-7-6x \\\\ -6 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ -2x+5+2x & =-2x+5+2x \\\\ 5 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x+5=-2x+5$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-8 & =-2x-8 \\\\ -2x-8+2x & =-2x-8+2x \\\\ -8 & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x-8=-2x-8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-2x+5=-2x+5$; $-2x-8=-2x-8$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6383f1f4-b0b1-5009-9a35-e129902b334b",
