    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e68af1a6-2891-54dc-9ab7-0abd9ba939d3",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x-4 & =-4x+4 \\\\ -4x-4+4x & =-4x+4+4x \\\\ -4 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =3x \\\\ 3x-3x & =3x-3x \\\\ 0 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x=3x$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x-1-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x-1=2x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3x=3x$; $2x-1=2x-1$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
