  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "75798c19-1184-5461-b4f1-94b60ccd0ad2",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+9 & =3x+9 \\\\ 3x+9-3x & =3x+9-3x \\\\ 9 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x+9=3x+9$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-2 & =7x+8 \\\\ 7x-2-7x & =7x+8-7x \\\\ -2 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+8 & =5x-8 \\\\ 4x+8-4x & =5x-8-4x \\\\ 8 & =x-8 \\\\ 8+8 & =x-8+8 \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $4x+8=5x-8$ has the unique solution $x=16.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3x+9=3x+9$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
