  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186110719,
      "id": "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca",
      "title": "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "64b61c51-7f95-5a63-afce-9fca099c5110",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+8 & =-x+8 \\\\ -x+8+x & =-x+8+x \\\\ 8 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-x+8=-x+8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =5x-1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =5x-1-4x \\\\ 1 & =x-1 \\\\ 1+1 & =x-1+1 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $4x+1=5x-1$ has the unique solution $x=2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =2x+3 \\\\ 2x+3-2x & =2x+3-2x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x+3=2x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $-x+8=-x+8$; $2x+3=2x+3$ only.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
